#### 1. Introduction

The purpose of this study is to explore the relationship between the use of social media and the development of social anxiety disorder among adolescents. Social media has become a ubiquitous part of modern life, with many people spending hours each day scrolling through their feeds and interacting with others online. While social media can be a valuable tool for connecting with others and building communities, it can also be a source of anxiety and stress for some individuals.

Social anxiety disorder is a mental health condition characterized by excessive fear and anxiety in social situations. People with social anxiety disorder may avoid social situations altogether or experience intense anxiety when they are forced to interact with others. This can lead to feelings of isolation and loneliness, as well as difficulties in forming and maintaining relationships.

While social anxiety disorder is a complex condition that cannot be attributed solely to social media use, it is possible that the use of social media may contribute to the development of social anxiety disorder in some individuals. For example, social media can create unrealistic expectations about what social interactions should look like, leading to feelings of inadequacy and anxiety when individuals do not meet these expectations. Additionally, social media can provide a platform for bullying and harassment, which can exacerbate feelings of anxiety and isolation.

The purpose of this study is to explore the relationship between social media use and social anxiety disorder among adolescents. By examining the ways in which social media use impacts social anxiety, we hope to gain a better understanding of this complex condition and develop interventions that can help individuals who are struggling with social anxiety.

#### 2. Literature Review

There is a growing body of research that suggests a link between social media use and social anxiety disorder. One study found that individuals who spent more time on social media were more likely to report symptoms of social anxiety disorder than those who spent less time on social media (Chou & Edge, 2012). Another study found that social media use was associated with increased levels of anxiety and depression, particularly among young adults (Lin et al., 2016).

However, the relationship between social media use and social anxiety disorder is not entirely clear. Some studies have found that social media use can actually help reduce symptoms of social anxiety disorder, particularly when used as a tool for social support (Holtgraves, 2011). Additionally, the type of social media used may play a role in the relationship between social media use and social anxiety disorder. For example, a study found that individuals who used Facebook more frequently reported lower levels of social anxiety than those who used other social media platforms (Kross et al., 2013).

Overall, the literature suggests that social media use may be associated with social anxiety disorder, but the relationship is complex and influenced by a variety of factors. Further research is needed to better understand the ways in which social media use impacts social anxiety and to develop interventions that can help individuals who are struggling with social anxiety.

#### 3. Methodology

This study will use a mixed-methods approach to explore the relationship between social media use and social anxiety disorder among adolescents. The study will consist of two phases: a quantitative survey and a qualitative focus group.

The survey will be administered to a sample of adolescents aged 13-18. Participants will be recruited through schools and community organizations. The survey will include questions about social media use, social anxiety symptoms, and demographic information. The survey will be administered online and will take approximately 20-30 minutes to complete.

The focus group will consist of a small group of adolescents who have reported high levels of social anxiety symptoms. Participants will be recruited through the survey and will be invited to participate in a focus group discussion. The focus group will be facilitated by a trained moderator and will be audio recorded. The focus group will last approximately 1-2 hours and will be held in a private setting.

#### 4. Data Analysis

The survey data will be analyzed using descriptive statistics and inferential statistics. Descriptive statistics will be used to summarize the data and identify patterns and trends. Inferential statistics will be used to test hypotheses and determine the significance of the relationships between variables.

The focus group data will be analyzed using thematic analysis. Thematic analysis involves identifying and categorizing patterns and themes in the data. The focus group data will be transcribed and coded, and themes will be identified based on the content of the transcripts.

#### 5. Expected Outcomes

The expected outcomes of this study are as follows:

1. To explore the relationship between social media use and social anxiety disorder among adolescents.

2. To identify the ways in which social media use impacts social anxiety symptoms.

3. To develop interventions that can help individuals who are struggling with social anxiety.

Overall, this study aims to contribute to our understanding of the complex relationship between social media use and social anxiety disorder. By exploring the ways in which social media use impacts social anxiety, we hope to develop interventions that can help individuals who are struggling with social anxiety and improve their quality of life.

#### 6. Conclusion

Social media has become an integral part of modern life, with many individuals spending hours each day scrolling through their feeds and interacting with others online. While social media can be a valuable tool for connecting with others and building communities, it can also be a source of anxiety and stress for some individuals. Social anxiety disorder is a mental health condition characterized by excessive fear and anxiety in social situations. While social anxiety disorder is a complex condition that cannot be attributed solely to social media use, it is possible that the use of social media may contribute to the development of social anxiety disorder in some individuals. This study aims to explore the relationship between social media use and social anxiety disorder among adolescents. By examining the ways in which social media use impacts social anxiety, we hope to gain a better understanding of this complex condition and develop interventions that can help individuals who are struggling with social anxiety.</s>